{"book": "gate-da", "num": 20, "label": "20", "slug": "20-calculus-functions", "title": "Functions", "tag": "Calculus & Optimization", "summary": "Given two sets $A$ and $B$, a relation from $A$ to $B$ is a set of ordered pairs $(x, y)$, where $x \\in A$ and $y \\in B$. A relation from $A$ to $B$ defines a relationship between these two sets.", "wordCount": 1327, "readingTimeMin": 7, "intro": [], "sections": [{"id": "20.1", "title": "Introduction", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "Given two sets $A$ and $B$, a <b>relation</b> from $A$ to $B$ is a set of ordered pairs $(x, y)$, where $x \\in A$ and $y \\in B$. A relation from $A$ to $B$ defines a relationship between these two sets."}, {"t": "p", "x": "A <b>function</b> is a special type of relation in which each element of the first set is related to <b>exactly one</b> element of the second set. The element of the first set is called the <b>input</b>, and the element of the second set is called the <b>output</b>. Functions are used extensively in mathematics to describe relationships between sets."}, {"t": "p", "x": "For any function, when we know the input, the output is determined, so we say that the output is a <b>function of the input</b>. For example:"}, {"t": "list", "ordered": false, "style": null, "desc": false, "items": [{"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "The area of a square is determined by its side length. Area (output) is a function of side length (input)."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "The velocity of a ball thrown in the air can be described as a function of the amount of time the ball is in the air."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "The cost of mailing a package is a function of the weight of the package."}]}]}, {"t": "box", "kind": "definition", "name": "Definition", "num": null, "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "A <b>function</b> $f$ consists of a set of inputs, a set of outputs, and a rule for assigning each input to exactly one output. The set of inputs is called the <b>domain</b> of the function. The set of outputs is called the <b>range</b> of the function."}]}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "1", "title": "Squaring function", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "Consider the function $f$ where the domain is the set of all real numbers and the rule is to square the input. Then, the input $x=3$ is assigned to the output $3^2 = 9$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "Since every nonnegative real number has a real square root, every nonnegative number is in the range of this function. Negative numbers are not in the range. Hence, the range is the set of nonnegative real numbers."}]}, {"t": "p", "x": "For a general function $f$ with domain $D$, we often denote:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "x = \\text{input (independent variable)}, \\quad y = \\text{output (dependent variable)}, \\quad y = f(x)"}, {"t": "p", "x": "For example, the squaring function can be written as $f(x) = x^2$."}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Visualizing a Function", "id": "visualizing-a-function"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig001.png"], "caption": ""}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>A function can be visualized as an input/output device.</b>"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig002.png"], "caption": ""}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>A function maps every element in the domain to exactly one element in the range. Two inputs may map to the same output.</b>"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig003.png"], "caption": ""}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>Graph of a function $f$ with domain $\\{1,2,3\\}$ and range $\\{1,2\\}$.</b>"}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Graph of a Function", "id": "graph-of-a-function"}, {"t": "p", "x": "A function can also be visualized by plotting points $(x, y)$ where $y=f(x)$ in the coordinate plane."}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "2", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "Consider $f(x) = 3 - x$ with domain $D = \\{1,2,3\\}$. The graph consists of points $(x, f(x))$:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "(1,2), (2,1), (3,0)"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig004.png"], "caption": "Graph of the function $f$ with $f(x)=3-x$ and domain $\\{1,2,3\\}$."}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Domain and Range", "id": "domain-and-range"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Every function has a domain. If a function is given by an equation with no specified domain, the domain is taken to be all real numbers for which $f(x)$ is real."}, {"t": "list", "ordered": false, "style": null, "desc": false, "items": [{"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "For $f(x)=x^2$, the domain is all real numbers."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "For $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$, the domain is all nonnegative real numbers."}]}]}, {"t": "p", "x": "We can describe infinite sets using <b>set-builder</b> or <b>interval notation</b>:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "\\{x \\mid 1 < x < 5\\} = (1,5)"}, {"t": "math", "x": "[1,5] = \\{x \\mid 1 \\le x \\le 5\\}"}, {"t": "math", "x": "[0,\\infty) = \\{x \\mid 0 \\le x\\}"}, {"t": "math", "x": "(-\\infty,0] = \\{x \\mid x \\le 0\\}"}, {"t": "math", "x": "(-\\infty, \\infty) = \\{x \\mid x \\text{ is any real number}\\}"}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Piecewise-Defined Functions", "id": "piecewise-defined-functions"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Some functions are defined by different rules for different parts of the domain. These are called piecewise-defined functions:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f(x) =\n\\begin{cases}\n3x+1, & x \\ge 2 \\\\\nx^2, & x < 2\n\\end{cases}"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "3", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "Evaluate $f(x)$ for $x=5$ and $x=-1$:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f(5) = 3(5)+1 = 16, \\quad f(-1) = (-1)^2 = 1"}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Open and Closed Intervals", "id": "open-and-closed-intervals"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "theorem", "name": "Concept", "num": null, "title": "Open and Closed Intervals", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "In mathematics, an <b>interval</b> is a set of real numbers lying between two endpoints. Intervals can be classified as:"}, {"t": "list", "ordered": false, "style": null, "desc": false, "items": [{"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "<b>Open interval</b> $(a,b)$: Includes all numbers strictly between $a$ and $b$, but <b>does not include</b> the endpoints $a$ and $b$.\nExample: $(1,5) = \\{ x \\mid 1 < x < 5 \\}$."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "<b>Closed interval</b> $[a,b]$: Includes all numbers between $a$ and $b$, <b>including</b> the endpoints $a$ and $b$.\nExample: $[1,5] = \\{ x \\mid 1 \\le x \\le 5 \\}$."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "<b>Half-open (or half-closed) intervals</b>:"}, {"t": "list", "ordered": false, "style": null, "desc": false, "items": [{"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "$[a,b)$ includes $a$ but not $b$."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "$(a,b]$ includes $b$ but not $a$."}]}]}]}]}]}]}, {"id": "20.2", "title": "Classes of Functions", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "Functions can be categorized into several types depending on their properties and rules of assignment. Understanding these classes helps in analyzing functions effectively."}, {"t": "box", "kind": "theorem", "name": "Concept", "num": null, "title": "Concept: Classes of Functions", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "Some common classes of functions include:"}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "1. Linear Functions", "id": "1-linear-functions"}, {"t": "p", "x": "A <b>linear function</b> has the form:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f(x) = mx + b"}, {"t": "p", "x": "where $m$ and $b$ are real constants. The graph of a linear function is a straight line."}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "4", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{}\nConsider $f(x) = 2x + 1$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>Domain:</b> all real numbers $\\mathbb{R}$\n<b>Range:</b> all real numbers $\\mathbb{R}$"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig005.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "2. Quadratic Functions", "id": "2-quadratic-functions"}, {"t": "p", "x": "A <b>quadratic function</b> has the form:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c, \\quad a \\neq 0"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Its graph is a parabola."}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "5", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{}\nConsider $f(x) = x^2 - 2x + 1$.\n<b>Domain:</b> all real numbers $\\mathbb{R}$\n<b>Range:</b> $[0, \\infty)$"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig006.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "3. Polynomial Functions", "id": "3-polynomial-functions"}, {"t": "p", "x": "A <b>polynomial function</b> is of the form:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f(x) = a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \\dots + a_1 x + a_0"}, {"t": "p", "x": "where $a_i$ are constants and $n$ is a non-negative integer."}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "6", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{}\nConsider $f(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 2x$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>Domain:</b> all real numbers $\\mathbb{R}$\n<b>Range:</b> all real numbers $\\mathbb{R}$"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig007.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "4. Rational Functions", "id": "4-rational-functions"}, {"t": "p", "x": "A <b>rational function</b> is the ratio of two polynomials:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f(x) = \\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}, \\quad Q(x) \\neq 0"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "7", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{}\nConsider $f(x) = \\frac{x+1}{x-1}$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>Domain:</b> all real numbers except $x=1$\n<b>Range:</b> all real numbers except $y=1$"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig008.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "5. Exponential Functions", "id": "5-exponential-functions"}, {"t": "p", "x": "An <b>exponential function</b> has the form:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f(x) = a^x, \\quad a > 0, a \\neq 1"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "8", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{}\nConsider $f(x) = 2^x$.\n<b>Domain:</b> all real numbers $\\mathbb{R}$\n<b>Range:</b> $(0, \\infty)$"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig009.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "6. Logarithmic Functions", "id": "6-logarithmic-functions"}, {"t": "p", "x": "A <b>logarithmic function</b> is the inverse of an exponential function:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f(x) = \\log_a x, \\quad a > 0, a \\neq 1"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "9", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{}\nConsider $f(x) = \\log_2 x$.\n<b>Domain:</b> $(0, \\infty)$\n<b>Range:</b> all real numbers $\\mathbb{R}$"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig010.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "7. Trigonometric Functions", "id": "7-trigonometric-functions"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Trigonometric functions like sine, cosine, and tangent are fundamental periodic functions."}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "10", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{}\nConsider $f(x) = \\sin x$.\n<b>Domain:</b> all real numbers $\\mathbb{R}$\n<b>Range:</b> $[-1,1]$"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig011.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "8. Absolute Value Function", "id": "8-absolute-value-function"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "definition", "name": "Definition", "num": null, "title": "Definition: Absolute Value Function", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "The <b>absolute value function</b> $f(x) = |x|$ is defined as"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f(x) =\n\\begin{cases}\n-x, & x < 0,\\\\\nx, & x \\ge 0.\n\\end{cases}"}]}, {"t": "list", "ordered": false, "style": null, "desc": false, "items": [{"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "The function outputs the distance of $x$ from 0 on the real number line."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "For negative inputs, it returns $-x$ (makes it positive)."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "For zero and positive inputs, it returns $x$ (same value)."}]}]}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "11", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{}\nGraphing $f(x) = |x|$"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig012.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Properties of $|x|$", "id": "properties-of-x"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "theorem", "name": "Concept", "num": null, "title": "Properties", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "style": null, "desc": false, "items": [{"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "$|x| \\ge 0$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "$|x| = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "$|ab| = |a||b|$, $|a/b| = |a|/|b|$ for $b\\neq 0$."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "$|a+b| \\le |a| + |b|$ (Triangle Inequality)."}]}]}]}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "12", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Evaluating $|x|$}"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f(-5) = |-5| = 5, \\quad f(3) = |3| = 3, \\quad f(0) = |0| = 0"}]}]}, {"id": "20.3", "title": "Types of Functions", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "Functions can be classified based on how elements in the domain and range are related. Here are some important types:"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "theorem", "name": "Concept", "num": null, "title": "Concept: Types of Functions", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "style": null, "desc": false, "items": [{"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "<b>One-to-One (Injective)</b>: Each element of the domain maps to a <i>unique</i> element of the range."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "<b>Onto (Surjective)</b>: Every element of the range has at least one element of the domain mapping to it."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "<b>Bijective</b>: Function is both one-to-one and onto. Such functions have inverses."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "<b>Many-to-One</b>: Two or more elements of the domain map to the same element in the range."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "<b>Constant Function</b>: All inputs map to the same output."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "<b>Identity Function</b>: Each element maps to itself."}]}]}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "1. One-to-One Function (Injective)", "id": "1-one-to-one-function-injective"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "13", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{One-to-One Function}\nConsider $f(x) = 2x + 1$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>Domain:</b> $\\mathbb{R}$, <b>Range:</b> $\\mathbb{R}$"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Check horizontal line test: every horizontal line intersects the graph exactly once, so $f$ is one-to-one."}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig013.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "2. Onto Function (Surjective)", "id": "2-onto-function-surjective"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "14", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Onto Function}\nConsider $f(x) = x^3$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>Domain:</b> $\\mathbb{R}$, <b>Range:</b> $\\mathbb{R}$\nEvery $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$ has a solution $x = \\sqrt[3]{y}$, so $f$ is onto."}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig014.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "3. Bijective Function", "id": "3-bijective-function"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "15", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Bijective Function}\nConsider $f(x) = x + 3$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "- One-to-one: Yes, each input gives unique output.\n- Onto: Yes, every $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is covered."}, {"t": "p", "x": "Hence, $f$ is bijective and has an inverse $f^{-1}(x) = x - 3$."}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig015.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "4. Many-to-One Function", "id": "4-many-to-one-function"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "16", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Many-to-One Function}\nConsider $f(x) = x^2$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "- $f(2) = f(-2) = 4$\n- Multiple inputs map to same output, so it is many-to-one.\n- Graph fails horizontal line test."}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig016.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "5. Constant Function", "id": "5-constant-function"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "17", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Constant Function}\nConsider $f(x) = 5$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "- All inputs map to the same output.\n- Graph is a horizontal line."}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig017.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "6. Identity Function", "id": "6-identity-function"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "18", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Identity Function}\nConsider $f(x) = x$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "- Each input maps to itself.\n- Graph is a straight line passing through the origin with slope 1."}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig018.png"], "caption": ""}]}]}, {"id": "20.4", "title": "Inverse Function", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "An inverse function reverses the operation done by a particular function. Whatever a function does, the inverse function undoes it."}, {"t": "box", "kind": "definition", "name": "Definition", "num": null, "title": "Definition: Inverse Function", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "Given a function $f$ with domain $D$ and range $R$, its <b>inverse function</b> (if it exists) is the function $f^{-1}$ with domain $R$ and range $D$ such that"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f^{-1}(y) = x \\quad \\text{if and only if} \\quad f(x) = y."}, {"t": "p", "x": "Equivalently,"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f^{-1}(f(x)) = x \\quad \\text{for all } x \\in D, \\quad f(f^{-1}(y)) = y \\quad \\text{for all } y \\in R."}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Example of an Inverse Function", "id": "example-of-an-inverse-function"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "19", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Finding an Inverse}\nLet $f(x) = x^3 + 4$. Solve $y = x^3 + 4$ for $x$:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "x = \\sqrt[3]{y - 4}"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Define $f^{-1}(y) = \\sqrt[3]{y - 4}$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "Verification:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f^{-1}(f(x)) = \\sqrt[3]{(x^3 + 4) - 4} = x"}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "One-to-One Functions", "id": "one-to-one-functions"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "definition", "name": "Definition", "num": null, "title": "Definition: One-to-One Function", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "A function $f$ is <b>one-to-one</b> if"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f(x_1) \\neq f(x_2) \\quad \\text{whenever} \\quad x_1 \\neq x_2"}]}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>Horizontal Line Test:</b> A function is one-to-one if every horizontal line intersects its graph at most once."}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "20", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{}"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig019.png"], "caption": ""}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig020.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Finding an Inverse Function", "id": "finding-an-inverse-function"}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>Strategy:</b> Solve $y=f(x)$ for $x$, then interchange $x$ and $y$:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "y = f^{-1}(x)"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "21", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Finding an Inverse}\nFind the inverse of $f(x) = 3x - 4$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>Solution:</b>"}, {"t": "math", "x": "y = 3x - 4 \\implies x = \\frac{y+4}{3} \\implies f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{x+4}{3}"}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>Domain and Range:</b> Both are $\\mathbb{R}$. Verification:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f^{-1}(f(x)) = \\frac{3x-4+4}{3} = x"}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Graphing Inverse Functions", "id": "graphing-inverse-functions"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "theorem", "name": "Concept", "num": null, "title": "Concept: Graph of Inverse Function", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "The graph of $f^{-1}$ is the reflection of the graph of $f$ about the line $y=x$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "If $(a,b)$ is on $f$, then $(b,a)$ is on $f^{-1}$."}]}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "22", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Sketching Graph of an Inverse}"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig021.png"], "caption": ""}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Restricting Domains", "id": "restricting-domains"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Some functions are not one-to-one over their entire domain. By restricting the domain, we can define an inverse."}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "23", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Restricting Domain of $f(x)=x^2$}"}, {"t": "math", "x": "g(x) = x^2, \\quad x \\ge 0 \\implies g^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{x}, \\quad \\text{Domain } [0, \\infty)"}, {"t": "math", "x": "h(x) = x^2, \\quad x \\le 0 \\implies h^{-1}(x) = -\\sqrt{x}, \\quad \\text{Domain } (-\\infty, 0]"}]}]}, {"id": "20.5", "title": "Composite Functions", "blocks": [{"t": "box", "kind": "definition", "name": "Definition", "num": null, "title": "Definition: Composite Function", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "Given two functions $f$ and $g$, the <b>composite function</b> $f \\circ g$ is defined by"}, {"t": "math", "x": "(f \\circ g)(x) = f(g(x))"}, {"t": "p", "x": "for all $x$ in the domain of $g$ such that $g(x)$ lies in the domain of $f$."}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Example of Composite Function", "id": "example-of-composite-function"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "24", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Composite Function Example}\nLet $f(x) = x^2$ and $g(x) = x+1$. Then,"}, {"t": "math", "x": "(f \\circ g)(x) = f(g(x)) = f(x+1) = (x+1)^2"}, {"t": "math", "x": "(g \\circ f)(x) = g(f(x)) = g(x^2) = x^2 + 1"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Notice that in general, $f \\circ g \\neq g \\circ f$."}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Rules of Composite Functions", "id": "rules-of-composite-functions"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "theorem", "name": "Concept", "num": null, "title": "Rules of Composite Functions", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": true, "style": null, "desc": false, "items": [{"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "<b>Associative Law:</b> $(f \\circ g) \\circ h = f \\circ (g \\circ h)$"}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "<b>Domain Rule:</b> The domain of $f \\circ g$ consists of all $x$ in the domain of $g$ such that $g(x)$ is in the domain of $f$."}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "<b>Non-commutative:</b> In general, $f \\circ g \\neq g \\circ f$."}]}]}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Example: Associative Law", "id": "example-associative-law"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "25", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Associativity}\nLet $f(x) = x^2$, $g(x) = x+1$, and $h(x) = 2x$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "Compute $(f \\circ g) \\circ h$:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "(f \\circ g) \\circ h (x) = f(g(h(x))) = f((2x)+1) = (2x+1)^2"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Compute $f \\circ (g \\circ h)$:"}, {"t": "math", "x": "f \\circ (g \\circ h)(x) = f(g(h(x))) = f((2x)+1) = (2x+1)^2"}, {"t": "p", "x": "Hence, $(f \\circ g) \\circ h = f \\circ (g \\circ h)$"}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Domain of Composite Functions", "id": "domain-of-composite-functions"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "26", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Domain Example}\nLet $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$, $g(x) = x-1$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "Then $(f \\circ g)(x) = f(g(x)) = \\sqrt{x-1}$."}, {"t": "p", "x": "<b>Domain:</b> $x-1 \\ge 0 \\implies x \\ge 1$, so the domain is $[1, \\infty)$"}]}, {"t": "h", "level": 3, "x": "Graphical Representation", "id": "graphical-representation"}, {"t": "box", "kind": "example", "name": "Example", "num": "27", "title": null, "blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "{Graph of Composite Function}"}, {"t": "fig", "num": null, "figs": [], "srcs": ["/books/gate-da/calc/fig022.png"], "caption": ""}, {"t": "list", "ordered": false, "style": null, "desc": false, "items": [{"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "Blue: $(f \\circ g)(x) = (x+1)^2$"}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "Green dashed: $g(x) = x+1$"}]}, {"blocks": [{"t": "p", "x": "Red dashed: $f(x) = x^2$"}]}]}]}]}], "sectionCount": 5}